<header><p class="st-kicker">protectorate · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Unfederated Malay States</h1><p class="st-meta">1909 CE – 1946 CE</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Collective name for five Malay sultanates under British protection that kept more self-rule than the federated states.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-siamese-treaty-of-1909">Anglo-Siamese Treaty of 1909</a> transferred Siamese rights over the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kedah-sultanate">Kedah Sultanate</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kelantan-sultanate">Kelantan</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/terengganu-sultanate">Terengganu</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/perlis">Perlis</a> to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a>, which installed British advisers. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/johor-sultanate">Johor</a>, long independent under modernising rulers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-bakar-of-johor">Abu Bakar</a>, accepted a general adviser in 1914. These five states, which never joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/federated-malay-states">Federated Malay States</a>, were known as the Unfederated Malay States.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>There was no common government: each sultan kept his own administration staffed largely by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malays">Malays</a>, with a British adviser whose advice had to be followed except in religion and custom. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">Rice</a> farming and fishing dominated the northern states, while <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/johor-sultanate">Johor</a> developed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a> and pineapple plantations.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> occupied them from 1942, handing <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kedah-sultanate">Kedah</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/perlis">Perlis</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kelantan-sultanate">Kelantan</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/terengganu-sultanate">Terengganu</a> to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/thailand">Thailand</a> in 1943. In 1946 <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> merged them into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/malayan-union">Malayan Union</a>, replaced in 1948 by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/federation-of-malaya">Federation of Malaya</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/unfederated-malay-states.jpg" alt="Unfederated Malay States" width="320" height="344"><figcaption>Image: Lilauid (talk · contribs) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:British_Malaya_circa_1922_en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1909 CE – 1946 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>protectorate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ibrahim of Johor</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Malay</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/malaysia">Malaysia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>UMS</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1928&amp;focus=unfederated-malay-states" aria-label="Unfederated Malay States on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7837 2087 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Unfederated Malay States lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7881 2378h0M7780 2205h0M7776 2191h0M7833 2204h0M7859 2234h0M4997 651h0M7823 2316h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8157 2343h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M8157 2343h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Malaysia. <a href="/atlas?year=1928&amp;focus=unfederated-malay-states">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
